From b2234a713743d4433bd09f92430edaf6eec27238 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kayori <thatzokay@proton.me>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 01:23:45 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 31/33] grate: compile POW, EX2, LG2, RSQ and SQRT in the fragment
 shader

Firefox (WebRender) aborted the whole process on the tablet:

  GRATE FRAG TGSI UNIMPLEMENTED: 0x1e
  Assertion failed: 0 (fp/tgsi.c: emit_tgsi_instr)

0x1e is TGSI_OPCODE_POW. The fragment compiler only knew RCP among the
scalar special-function ops, although the SFU implements more. Measured on
the tablet with a hook that put an SFU op on a computed value:

* RCP, RSQ, SQRT and LG2 are one SFU op each (within 1/255 of libm).
* EX2 needs PREEX2 first. PREEX2 returns 2^floor(v) * (1 + frac(v)); the
  EX2 after it takes the row PREEX2 wrote as its register and corrects the
  mantissa to 2^frac(v). Going through an ALU MOV in between does not work.
* The multiplier that carries the SFU result can take a row register as its
  second factor, so POW(x, y) is EX2(PREEX2(y * LG2(x))) with y in a row.

emit_RCP becomes emit_SFU, built from emit_load_scalar (the operand copy)
and emit_sfu_stage (one MFU instruction). RCP produces the same
instructions as before; RSQ takes the magnitude of its operand as TGSI
defines it. A saturated result is now stored through the copy instead of
being left to the multiplier, which ignored the modifier.

tools/gpu/grate-pow-test.c checks ten expressions against libm on the
tablet (worst 1/255): pow with a constant, uniform and varying exponent, a
varying base, an sRGB-shaped computed operand, exp2, log2, inversesqrt
and sqrt. The existing fragment, blend, texture and stencil tests give the
same output as before.
